"""Standalone mathematical exposition of the ULA and IPLA entropy arguments.

The statements, analytic assumptions, and proved conclusions are recorded
separately. References identify the supporting formal statements.
"""


def ref(file,name):
    return dict(file=file,name=name)


def part(identifier,title,body,references=(),note=''):
    return dict(id=identifier,title=title,body=body,references=list(references),note=note)


def node(identifier,row,col,file,title,detail,parents=(),final=False):
    return dict(id=identifier,row=row,col=col,file=file,title=title,detail=detail,parents=parents,final=final)


ULA_STATEMENT=r'''<p>Let \(d\ge1\) and let \(\nu(dx)=Z^{-1}e^{-U(x)}dx\) be a probability on \(\mathbb R^d\). Assume \(U\) is differentiable, its gradient is globally \(L\)-Lipschitz with \(L>0\), and \(\nu\) satisfies the logarithmic Sobolev inequality with constant \(\alpha>0\):</p>\[2\alpha H_\nu(\rho)\le I_\nu(\rho),\qquad
H_\nu(\rho)=\int\log\frac{d\rho}{d\nu}\,d\rho,\quad
I_\nu(\rho)=\int\left\|\nabla\log\frac{d\rho}{d\nu}\right\|^2d\rho.\]<p>Let initial law \(\rho_0\) have finite relative entropy. Let \(Z_k\) be mutually independent standard Gaussian vectors, independent of initialization, and define</p>\[X_{k+1}=X_k-h\nabla U(X_k)+\sqrt{2h}\,Z_k,
\qquad 0&lt;h\le\frac{\alpha}{4L^2},\]<p>Then the laws \(\rho_k=\operatorname{Law}(X_k)\) have finite relative entropy and for every \(k\ge0\) satisfy</p>\[\boxed{H_\nu(\rho_k)\le e^{-\alpha hk}H_\nu(\rho_0)+\frac{8hdL^2}{\alpha}.}\]<p>This is the bound in <a href="https://www.cs.yale.edu/homes/wibisono/VW19.pdf">Vempala–Wibisono (2019), Theorem 2</a>. Its first term decays with iteration, while its second term is the time-discretization contribution. Convexity of \(U\) is not required. The inequality \(\alpha\le L\) follows below from the functional inequality and smoothness in positive dimension. For infinite initial entropy the corresponding extended-valued inequality is immediate; the finite-entropy assertion supplies the quantitative conclusion.</p>'''


ULA_PROOF=[
part('entropy','The Gibbs relative density and Gaussian Euler interpolation',r'''<p>Write \(\nu(dy)=J(y)dy\) with \(J=Z^{-1}e^{-U}>0\). A positive continuously differentiable relative density \(r\) satisfying \(\int r\,d\nu=1\) defines probability \(r\nu\). Its logarithmic Sobolev inequality is</p>\[2\alpha\,\mathrm{KL}(r\nu\mid\nu)\le\int\|\nabla\log r\|^2r\,d\nu.\]<p>The entropy and Fisher information are initially extended nonnegative quantities. Their finiteness will follow for the Gaussian interpolation, whose positive continuously differentiable relative density belongs to this test class.</p><p>Take \(X\sim\mu\) independent of \(Z_0\sim\mathcal N(0,I_d)\) and define</p>\[Y_t=X-t\nabla U(X)+\sqrt{2t}\,Z_0,\quad\rho_t=\operatorname{Law}(Y_t).\]<p>The initial law is \(\mu\), and at time \(h\) the law is its Gaussian Euler transition. Repeated transitions give the discrete laws. If \(p_t\) is the volume density of \(\rho_t\), the relative density is \(r_t=p_t/J\). Thus \(\log r_t=\log p_t+U+\log Z\) and its score is \(S_t=\nabla\log p_t+\nabla U\). For the Gibbs law these gradient terms cancel, giving zero Fisher information. The inequality therefore concerns relative density variation rather than variation of \(p_t\) alone.</p><p>The drift during this interpolation is fixed at its initial value. Its discrepancy from the Langevin drift is the difference of \(\nabla U(X)\) and \(\nabla U(Y_t)\). Estimating that difference gives a continuous entropy inequality whose two endpoint laws are exactly the two discrete laws needed for the recurrence.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/FunctionalInequality.lean','IsLogSobolev'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/ULALaw.lean','interpolationLaw_endpoint')],
'The interpolated relative density is positive and continuously differentiable at every positive time, so it belongs to the stated test class.'),

part('compatibility','The inequality α ≤ L from bounded tests',r'''<p>For bounded continuously differentiable \(f\) with \(\|\nabla f\|\le D\), let \(\Lambda(s)=\log\int e^{sf}d\nu\). Density \(e^{sf-\Lambda(s)}\) is an admissible logarithmic Sobolev test. Its logarithmic gradient is \(s\nabla f\) and its entropy is \(s\Lambda'(s)-\Lambda(s)\), so</p>\[2\alpha\bigl(s\Lambda'(s)-\Lambda(s)\bigr)\le s^2D^2.\]<p>Boundedness justifies differentiation near zero. Divide by \(s^2\) and let \(s\downarrow0\). The quotient \((s\Lambda'(s)-\Lambda(s))/s^2\) tends to \(\Lambda''(0)/2=\operatorname{Var}_\nu(f)/2\); the left side therefore tends to \(\alpha\operatorname{Var}_\nu(f)\). Hence \(\alpha\operatorname{Var}_\nu(f)\le D^2\).</p><p>Choose unit direction \(v\). Translation invariance and the smooth upper bound <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Analysis/Smooth.lean" data-report-declaration="smooth_upper_bound"></cite> give centered sub-Gaussian score \(g_v=\langle\nabla U,v\rangle\) by <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsScore.lean" data-report-declaration="gibbs_score_subgaussian"></cite>. Its second moment satisfies \(\int g_v^2d\nu\le L\) by <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsScore.lean" data-report-declaration="integral_sq_le_of_subgaussian"></cite>. For bounded continuously differentiable tests integration by parts gives</p>\[\int f g_v\,d\nu=\int\langle\nabla f,v\rangle d\nu.\]<p>Use \(f_t(x)=\sin(t\langle v,x\rangle)\). Its gradient is bounded by \(t\), and the identity is \(\int(f_t-\nu f_t)g_vd\nu=t\int\cos(t\langle v,x\rangle)d\nu\). Cauchy–Schwarz and the variance bound imply</p>\[\alpha\left(\int\cos(t\langle v,x\rangle)d\nu\right)^2\le L.\]<p>The bounded cosine tends pointwise to one, so dominated convergence gives \(\alpha\le L\). Explicitly, \(D=t\) gives \(\operatorname{Var}(f_t)\le t^2/\alpha\) and the centered score second moment is at most \(L\). Thus</p>\[t^2\left(\int\cos(t\langle v,x\rangle)d\nu\right)^2
=\left(\int(f_t-\nu f_t)g_vd\nu\right)^2
\le\frac{Lt^2}{\alpha}.\]<p>For \(t>0\) divide by \(t^2\) and multiply by \(\alpha\). The cosine limit needs no moment of \(\langle v,X\rangle\). Positive dimension is used only to choose a unit vector. On a singleton, all probability laws coincide and relative entropy is zero. No position second moment or Hessian assumption enters the positive-dimensional argument.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/Compatibility.lean','IsLogSobolev.exponential_test'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/Compatibility.lean','logSobolev_constant_le_smoothness')],
'The logarithmic Sobolev definition implies the compatibility inequality; no additional compatibility hypothesis is required.'),

part('moment','The initial score moment from relative entropy',r'''<p>The directional score estimate <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/GibbsScore.lean" data-report-declaration="gibbs_score_lmgf_le"></cite> states, for every \(v\),</p>\[\int e^{\langle\nabla U(x),v\rangle}d\nu(x)\le e^{L\|v\|^2/2}.\]<p>Average over an independent auxiliary centered Gaussian. Nonnegativity permits Tonelli, and completion of the Gaussian square at variance corresponding to \(4L\) gives</p>\[\int\exp\!\left(\frac{\|\nabla U\|^2}{4L}\right)d\nu
\le 2^{d/2}\le e^{d/2}.\]<p>For probability \(\mu\) with finite relative entropy, the entropy variational inequality gives</p>\[G_0:=\int\|\nabla U\|^2d\mu\le4L\,H_\nu(\mu)+2dL
\le\frac{4L^2}{\alpha}H_\nu(\mu)+2dL.\]<p>The second inequality uses \(\alpha\le L\) and nonnegativity of entropy. Integrability is proved before this real-valued calculation: for density \(a=d\mu/d\nu\), convex duality \(ab\le a\log a-a+e^b\) bounds the weighted nonnegative score-square integrand. Subtraction of the finite logarithmic moment bound gives the stated variational coefficient.</p><p>Indeed, for \(a=d\mu/d\nu\) and observable \(F\) with finite exponential moment, normalize \(e^F\nu\) by \(M=\int e^Fd\nu\). Nonnegativity of divergence against this tilted probability gives</p>\[0\le\operatorname{KL}\left(\mu\,\middle\Vert\,M^{-1}e^F\nu\right)
=H_\nu(\mu)-\int Fd\mu+\log M.\]<p>The convex-duality bound and monotone truncation justify the integrals for the full nonnegative \(F\). Substituting \(F=\|\nabla U\|^2/(4L)\) and \(\log M\le d/2\), then multiplying by \(4L\), yields the score moment. It is derived from finite entropy and does not require an initial position moment.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/EntropyMoment.lean','gibbs_score_exponential_moment'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/EntropyMoment.lean','gibbs_gradient_moment_bound_of_finite_kl_of_alpha_le')],
'The exponential score moment gives the needed integrability directly. No entropy–transport inequality is used.'),

part('gaussian-law','The displacement and gradient-error second moments',r'''<p>The interpolated displacement is</p>\[Y_t-X=-t\nabla U(X)+\sqrt{2t}\,Z_0.\]<p>Its mixed term has zero expectation by independence and Gaussian centering. Since \(\mathbb E\|Z_0\|^2=d\), expansion gives</p>\[\mathbb E\|Y_t-X\|^2=t^2G_0+2td.\]<p>Gradient Lipschitzness then implies</p>\[\mathbb E\|\nabla U(Y_t)-\nabla U(X)\|^2
\le L^2(t^2G_0+2td).\]<p>The score and Gaussian second moments make every term integrable. The joint law of \((X,Y_t)\) has density \(K_t(x,y)\mu(dx)dy\) with Gaussian transition kernel \(K_t\), so the same estimate holds in the kernel pair integral.</p><p>The cancellation is the justified identity</p>\[\mathbb E\langle\nabla U(X),Z_0\rangle
=\left\langle\mathbb E\nabla U(X),\mathbb EZ_0\right\rangle=0.\]<p>Cauchy–Schwarz gives mixed-term integrability. No bound on \(\mathbb E\|X\|^2\) is needed because position cancels before the expansion. Multiplication by \(L^2\) retains drift contribution \(t^2G_0\) and Gaussian contribution \(2td\). These give, respectively, the old-entropy and dimensional parts of the subsequent forcing.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianDrift.lean','frozen_displacement_second_moment'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianEnergyIntegrability.lean','frozenGaussianKernel_gradient_error_energy')],
'The Gaussian transition and its joint input–output law satisfy the stated energy estimate, with all terms integrable.'),

part('density-and-flux','Gaussian density regularity, finite Fisher information, and the weak equation',r'''<p>For \(t>0\) define</p>\[K_t(x,y)=(4\pi t)^{-d/2}e^{-\|y-x+t\nabla U(x)\|^2/(4t)},\quad
p_t(y)=\int K_t(x,y)d\mu(x),\quad r_t=p_t/J.\]<p>The first spatial Gaussian derivatives are bounded by Gaussian polynomials uniformly in their centers. These dominate differentiation under the probability integral. Thus \(p_t\) and \(r_t\) are positive continuously differentiable functions. The probability on \(x\) with weight proportional to \(K_t(x,y)\mu(dx)\) gives</p>\[\nabla\log p_t(y)=-\frac1{2t}\mathbb E[y-X+t\nabla U(X)\mid Y_t=y].\]<p>Posterior Jensen and the Gaussian moment yield \(\int\|\nabla\log p_t\|^2d\rho_t\le d/(2t)\). With \(S_t:=\nabla\log r_t=\nabla\log p_t+\nabla U\) this implies</p>\[I_t:=\int\|S_t\|^2d\rho_t\le d/t+2\int\|\nabla U\|^2d\rho_t&lt;\infty.\]<p>Gradient Lipschitzness and the displacement moment bound the remaining score moment by \(2(1+L^2t^2)G_0+4tdL^2\). The logarithmic Sobolev inequality therefore gives finite entropy for \(t>0\) and \(2\alpha H(t)\le I_t\).</p><p>Direct time differentiation gives</p>\[\dot K_t=K_t\left[-\frac d{2t}+\frac{\|y-x\|^2}{4t^2}-\frac{\|\nabla U(x)\|^2}{4}\right].\]<p>On compact positive-time intervals its absolute integral is bounded by \(G_0\) and Gaussian moments. Set \(F_t(x,y)=\nabla_y K_t(x,y)+K_t(x,y)\nabla U(x)\); its spatial divergence is \(\dot K_t\). Integration by parts applies to every bounded continuously differentiable \(\psi\) with \(\nabla\psi\in L^2(\rho_t)\) and gives</p>\[\int\dot p_t\psi\,dy=-\iint\langle F_t(x,y),\nabla\psi(y)\rangle d\mu(x)dy.\]<p>Thus the weak equation is proved for the interpolation. The score has a jointly measurable representative equal to \(S_t\) at positive times. The Fisher estimate follows explicitly from</p>\[\begin{aligned}
\int p_t(y)\|\nabla\log p_t(y)\|^2dy
&\le\iint\frac{\|y-x+t\nabla U(x)\|^2}{4t^2}
K_t(x,y)d\mu(x)dy\\
&=\frac{2td}{4t^2}=\frac d{2t}.
\end{aligned}\]<p>The inner moment is independent of the initial location, so no position moment of \(\mu\) is needed. Its divergence as \(t\downarrow0\) requires first working away from zero. In the weak equation the bounded test controls the flux-test integral, finite gradient energy controls the flux-gradient integral by Cauchy–Schwarz, and the Gaussian derivative formula controls the flux derivative. These integrability conditions justify global integration by parts without an assumed boundary-decay statement.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianEntropy.lean','interpolationLebesgue_fisher_bound'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianTimeDerivative.lean','hasDerivAt_interpolationLebesgueDensity_time'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianWeakEquation.lean','interpolationLebesgueDensity_weak_identity')],
'The kernel derivatives and finite score moment justify the Fisher estimate, weak equation, and integration by parts.'),

part('production','Bounded logarithmic tests and the entropy derivative',r'''<p>To justify a logarithmic test, for \(m\ge0\) define</p>\[\begin{aligned}
\chi_m(z)&=(m+1)\arctan\frac z{m+1},\\
w_m(z)&=\chi_m'(z)=\frac1{1+(z/(m+1))^2},\\
\Phi_m(r)&=\int_1^r\chi_m(\log u)du.
\end{aligned}\]<p>These satisfy \(0\le w_m\le1\), \(w_m\to1\), and \(0\le\Phi_m(r)\le r\log r-r+1\), with convergence to the latter upper bound. The bounded continuously differentiable test \(\psi_{m,t}=\chi_m(\log r_t)\) has gradient \(\nabla\psi_{m,t}=w_m(\log r_t)S_t\), square-integrable because \(I_t&lt;\infty\).</p><p>The time derivative of \(J\Phi_m(r_t)\) is \(\chi_m(\log r_t)\dot p_t\). Boundedness of \(\chi_m\) and the spacetime integrability of \(\dot p_t\) justify Fubini and the fundamental theorem on \([a,b]\subset(0,\infty)\). Apply the weak equation to identify this derivative with the weighted Gaussian pair flux. The Fisher and velocity energies are integrable on the interval, and Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the absolute mixed flux by their half-sum. Dominated convergence as \(m\to\infty\) then gives</p>\[H'(t)=-I_t+\mathbb E\langle\nabla U(Y_t)-\nabla U(X),S_t(Y_t)\rangle.\]<p>Fubini identifies the average component score with the mixture score, without choosing a time-dependent conditional drift. Applying \(\langle a,b\rangle\le\|a\|^2+\|b\|^2/4\) and the gradient-error energy gives</p>\[\boxed{H'(t)\le-\tfrac34I_t+t^2L^2G_0+2tdL^2}\quad\text{for almost every }t>0.\]<p>For entropy at a fixed time the Gibbs dominating function is \(r_t\log r_t-r_t+1\). For the time flux it is the spacetime energy bound. Passage to the two limits yields</p>\[H(b)-H(a)=\int_a^b\left[-I_t+
\mathbb E\langle\nabla U(Y_t)-\nabla U(X),S_t(Y_t)\rangle\right]dt.\]<p>The right side is integrable on every compact positive-time interval. Hence entropy is locally absolutely continuous and has the displayed derivative almost everywhere. Young’s inequality contributes one quarter of \(I_t\) to the error estimate, leaving three quarters as dissipation. No everywhere-defined entropy derivative is assumed.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/BoundedLog.lean','boundedEntropy_tendsto'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianEntropyIdentity.lean','interpolationEntropy_local_chain_rule'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GaussianEntropyProduction.lean','interpolationEntropy_derivative_bound_ae')],
'Bounded logarithmic tests and the two dominated limits give the positive-time entropy identity and its almost-everywhere derivative.'),

part('analytic-endpoints','The initial-time upper bound for finite-entropy laws',r'''<p>To pass to \(a\downarrow0\) in the positive-time inequality, use a one-sided initial entropy estimate. Data processing for the independent Gaussian transition gives</p>\[\mathrm{KL}(\mu P_t\mid\nu P_t)\le\mathrm{KL}(\mu\mid\nu).\]<p>For \(\delta=1-4tL>0\) put \(s=t/\delta\). The smooth upper bound <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Analysis/Smooth.lean" data-report-declaration="smooth_upper_bound"></cite> and gradient Lipschitzness give the completed-square inequality</p>\[U(y)-U(x)-\frac{\|y-x+t\nabla U(x)\|^2}{4t}
\le-\frac{\|x-y+s\nabla U(y)\|^2}{4s}
+\frac s4\|\nabla U(y)\|^2.\]<p>Integration in \(x\) and normalizer ratio \((s/t)^{d/2}=\delta^{-d/2}\) yield</p>\[\frac{d(\nu P_t)}{d\nu}(y)\le\delta^{-d/2}
\exp\!\left(\frac t{4\delta}\|\nabla U(y)\|^2\right).\]<p>The logarithmic likelihood-ratio identity and propagated gradient moment then give</p>\[H(t)\le M(t):=H(0)-\frac d2\log\delta
+\frac t{4\delta}\bigl[2(1+L^2t^2)G_0+4tdL^2\bigr].\]<p>The positive and negative likelihood-ratio parts are integrable before this addition of entropies. The majorant satisfies \(M(0)=H(0)\) and is continuous at zero. Thus the positive-time differential inequality may be integrated on \([a,h]\) and passed to \(a\downarrow0\). Finite initial relative entropy suffices, without an initial smooth density or smoothing of the potential.</p><p>For example suppose \(H'\le-aH+B\) with \(a>0\). Integration on \([s,h]\) with \(s>0\) gives</p>\[H(h)\le e^{-a(h-s)}H(s)+\frac Ba\bigl(1-e^{-a(h-s)}\bigr).\]<p>Substitute \(H(s)\le M(s)\) and let \(s\downarrow0\). Continuity of \(M\) gives the same upper bound with \(H(0)\) as the initial term. Only this upper majorant is needed; two-sided continuity of entropy at the initial time is not required.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/GibbsInterpolationBound.lean','gibbs_frozen_exponent_le'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/InitialEntropy.lean','interpolationLaw_entropy_eventually_le_majorant'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/IntegratedEntropy.lean','affine_decay_of_local_ae_deriv_le')],
'The one-sided initial-time majorant is derived for the Gaussian transition under finite initial entropy.'),

part('one-step','The one-step entropy estimate with coefficient six',r'''<p>Combine the logarithmic Sobolev inequality, initial score moment, and entropy derivative estimate:</p>\[H'\le-\tfrac32\alpha H+t^2L^2\left(\frac{4L^2}{\alpha}H(0)+2dL\right)+2tdL^2.\]<p>Conditions \(h\le\alpha/(4L^2)\) and \(\alpha\le L\) imply \(hL\le1/4\) and \(\alpha h\le1/4\). In particular</p>\[h^2L^2\frac{4L^2}{\alpha}\le\frac\alpha4,\qquad
h^2L^2(2dL)\le\tfrac12hdL^2.\]<p>For \(0&lt;t&lt;h\) the forcing is at most \(B=\alpha H(0)/4+(5/2)hdL^2\). Local absolute continuity permits the integrating-factor argument from \(a>0\), and the initial upper majorant permits \(a\downarrow0\). Therefore</p>\[H(h)\le e^{-3\alpha h/2}H(0)+\frac B{3\alpha/2}(1-e^{-3\alpha h/2})
\le e^{-3\alpha h/2}H(0)+Bh.\]<p>For \(0\le x\le1/4\) one has \(e^{-x}\ge e^{-3x/2}+x/4\). Substitute \(x=\alpha h\) and multiply by nonnegative \(H(0)\). This bounds the old-entropy forcing by the difference of exponentials. The remaining \((5/2)h^2dL^2\) is at most \(6h^2dL^2\), giving</p>\[\boxed{H_\nu(\mu P_h)\le e^{-\alpha h}H_\nu(\mu)+6h^2dL^2.}\]<p>Nonnegativity of \(H(0)\) justifies that multiplication, and nonnegativity of \(B\) bounds the integrated forcing by</p>\[\int_0^he^{-(3\alpha/2)(h-t)}Bdt\le Bh
=\frac{\alpha h}{4}H(0)+\frac52h^2dL^2.\]<p>Replacing coefficient \(e^{-3\alpha h/2}\) by \(e^{-\alpha h}\) includes the initial-entropy forcing. The remaining dimensional term is the discretization contribution. The coefficient six is a valid enlargement of \(5/2\) and is not claimed optimal. The argument only uses the entropy derivative almost everywhere at positive time.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/IntegratedEntropy.lean','one_step_entropy_of_ae_interpolation')],
'The preceding Gaussian interpolation arguments establish the analytic hypotheses used in this scalar comparison.'),

part('iteration','Finiteness of iterated entropy and the finite geometric sum',r'''<p>Inductively, finite current entropy gives a finite score moment; Gaussian interpolation gives finite relative Fisher information at positive time; and the logarithmic Sobolev inequality gives finite next entropy. Thus every \(H_k\) is an ordinary finite divergence.</p><p>Set \(q=e^{-\alpha h}\) and \(c=6h^2dL^2\). The one-step inequality and affine recurrence <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Recurrence.lean" data-report-declaration="affine_recurrence_exact"></cite> yield</p>\[H_k\le q^kH_0+c\frac{1-q^k}{1-q}.\]<p>For \(0\le x\le1/4\), the elementary exponential estimate gives</p>\[1-e^{-x}\ge\frac{x}{1+x}\ge\tfrac34x.\]<p>Use \(x=\alpha h\) and \(1-q^k\le1\) to obtain</p>\[\frac{6h^2dL^2}{1-e^{-\alpha h}}\le\frac{8hdL^2}{\alpha}.\]<p>Starting from \(H_0\), each occurrence of forcing \(c\) is multiplied by the contraction for its remaining steps. The finite-sum identity <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/Recurrence.lean" data-report-declaration="affine_recurrence_sum"></cite> is</p>\[H_k\le e^{-\alpha hk}H_0+
6h^2dL^2\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}e^{-\alpha hj}.\]<p>The denominator is positive because \(\alpha\) and \(h\) are positive. Its lower bound is proportional to \(h\), so local forcing of order \(h^2\) gives a global term of order \(h\). At fixed step, increasing \(k\) decreases only the initial contribution. Density regularity, moment bounds, the weak equation, entropy regularity, and compatibility were derived above, leaving smoothness, the target logarithmic Sobolev inequality, the step restriction, and finite initial entropy as the final hypotheses.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean','ula_kl_finite'),
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/DiscreteEntropy.lean','discrete_entropy_recurrence_bound')],
'The assumption check retains the stated mathematical hypotheses and the standard logical axioms of the formal system.')
]


IPLA_STATEMENT=r'''<p>Let \(N\ge1\) and \(D=d_\theta+Nd_x\) with \(D\ge1\). Assume differentiable joint potential \(U(\theta,x)\) has globally \(L\)-Lipschitz gradient and \(L>0\). Define the scaled potential and target by</p>\[z=(\theta,x^1/\sqrt N,\ldots,x^N/\sqrt N),\qquad
V_N(z)=\sum_{i=1}^NU(\theta,\sqrt N z_i),\quad
\Pi_N(dz)\propto e^{-V_N(z)}dz.\]<p>The Gibbs identity is <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Foundations.lean" data-report-declaration="gibbsTarget_eq_gibbsMeasure"></cite>. Assume its lifted target satisfies the logarithmic Sobolev inequality with constant \(\beta_N>0\). Compatibility <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/Compatibility.lean" data-report-declaration="logSobolev_constant_le_smoothness"></cite> gives \(\beta_N\le NL\) from these hypotheses. Take finite initial joint entropy, independent standard Gaussian innovations independent of initialization, and</p>\[0&lt;\gamma\le\frac{\beta_N}{4NL^2},\]<p>The laws \(\mu_k\) equal Gaussian Euler laws for \(V_N\) with step \(\gamma/N\) by <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/ULAAdapter.lean" data-report-declaration="laws_eq_ulaLawStep"></cite>. Their finite joint entropy satisfies</p>\[\frac{H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_k)}N\le
e^{-\beta_N\gamma k/N}\frac{H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_0)}N
+\frac{8\gamma DL^2}{\beta_N}.\]<p>When the lifted target has constant \(\beta_N=N\alpha\), this becomes</p>\[\boxed{\frac{H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_k)}N\le
e^{-\alpha\gamma k}\frac{H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_0)}N
+\frac{8\gamma L^2}{\alpha}\left(d_x+\frac{d_\theta}N\right).}\]<p>The step condition is then \(0&lt;\gamma\le\alpha/(4L^2)\). The functional inequality is assumed for the coupled lifted target itself; a one-pair inequality is insufficient for this substitution. Gaussian interpolation and moment estimates follow from <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean" data-report-declaration="ula_kl_bound"></cite> and finite initial joint entropy. If \(D=0\), the state space is a singleton and all probability-law divergences vanish.</p>'''


IPLA_PROOF=[
part('lift','Scaled particle geometry and the Gaussian transition',r'''<p>The physical pair \((\theta,x^i)\) is extracted as \((\theta,\sqrt N z_i)\) from the scaled state. The gradient formulas <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="gradient_potential_parameter"></cite> give the parameter-average and individual latent drifts at step \(\gamma/N\). The constants are</p>\[D=d_\theta+Nd_x,\qquad L_N=NL,\qquad h_N=\gamma/N.\]<p>The dimension follows from <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="finrank_state"></cite>. The parameter gradient sums all pair contributions and a latent derivative contributes \(\sqrt N\). Cauchy–Schwarz and the replicated-pair squared-norm identity give gradient constant \(NL\) as in <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="potential_gradient_lipschitz"></cite>. The independent Gaussian coordinate assumptions identify the transition laws by <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/ULAAdapter.lean" data-report-declaration="laws_eq_ulaLawStep"></cite>. The dimensions \(d_\theta\) and \(d_x\) need not coincide.</p><p>The physical parameter formula <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="scaledUpdate_parameter"></cite> is</p>\[\theta_{k+1}=\theta_k-\frac\gamma N\sum_i\nabla_\theta U(\theta_k,x_k^i)
+\sqrt{\frac{2\gamma}{N}}Z_k^\theta.\]<p>Multiplication of a latent scaled coordinate by \(\sqrt N\) gives the physical formula <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="scaledUpdate_particle"></cite>:</p>\[x_{k+1}^i=x_k^i-\gamma\nabla_xU(\theta_k,x_k^i)+\sqrt{2\gamma}Z_k^i.\]<p>The same joint innovations therefore realize the same particle algorithm. Its full dimension \(D\) and full joint entropy are the quantities used in the Gaussian Euler entropy theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean" data-report-declaration="ula_kl_bound"></cite>.</p>''',[
ref('IPLA/LSI/Theorem.lean','particleLaws_eq_laws')],
'The coordinate and probability-law identities identify this transition with the stated Gaussian particle recursion.'),
part('step','The lifted one-step entropy recurrence',r'''<p>Apply the one-step theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean" data-report-declaration="ula_kl_one_step"></cite> to \((V_N,\Pi_N)\) with logarithmic Sobolev constant \(\beta_N\), dimension \(D\), gradient constant \(NL\), and step \(\gamma/N\). The restriction is</p>\[\frac\gamma N\le\frac{\beta_N}{4(NL)^2}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\gamma\le\frac{\beta_N}{4NL^2}.\]<p>The forcing is</p>\[6h_N^2D L_N^2=6(\gamma/N)^2D(NL)^2=6\gamma^2DL^2.\]<p>Hence the full joint entropy satisfies</p>\[H_{k+1}\le e^{-\beta_N\gamma/N}H_k+6\gamma^2DL^2.\]<p>The logarithmic Sobolev hypothesis includes target normalization by <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/FunctionalInequality.lean" data-report-declaration="IsLogSobolev"></cite>. The finiteness statement <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean" data-report-declaration="ula_kl_finite"></cite> justifies real entropy at every iteration. The product \(\beta_Nh_N\) is \(\beta_N\gamma/N\). Step admissibility and compatibility give \(\beta_N\gamma/N\le1/4\) by <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/Compatibility.lean" data-report-declaration="logSobolev_constant_le_smoothness"></cite>, so the denominator estimate <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/DiscreteEntropy.lean" data-report-declaration="three_quarters_mul_le_one_sub_exp_neg"></cite> applies.</p><p>The recurrence is for full joint divergence. There is no factor \(N^{-1}\) in its forcing because the squared step contributes \(N^{-2}\) and squared gradient constant contributes \(N^2\). Division by \(N\) is a separate normalization of the entire inequality.</p>''',[
ref('UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean','ula_kl_one_step')],
'The lifted substitution preserves all constants in the Gaussian Euler entropy estimate.'),
part('normalization','Full and per-particle joint entropy estimates',r'''<p>The full entropy theorem <cite data-report-ref="UnadjustedLangevin/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean" data-report-declaration="ula_kl_bound"></cite> has lifted error term</p>\[\frac{8h_NDL_N^2}{\beta_N}
=\frac{8\gamma NDL^2}{\beta_N}.\]<p>Divide all terms by positive \(N\), including the initial entropy:</p>\[\frac{H_k}N\le e^{-\beta_N\gamma k/N}\frac{H_0}N
+\frac{8\gamma DL^2}{\beta_N}.\]<p>If the lifted target has constant \(\beta_N=N\alpha\), identity \(D/N=d_x+d_\theta/N\) gives rate \(e^{-\alpha\gamma k}\) and per-particle error \((8\gamma L^2/\alpha)(d_x+d_\theta/N)\). This premise concerns \(\Pi_N\), whose shared parameter prevents independent-pair tensorization.</p><p>Under \(\beta_N=N\alpha\) the full dimension \(D=d_\theta+Nd_x\) gives</p>\[\frac{8\gamma DL^2}{N\alpha}
=\frac{8\gamma L^2}{\alpha}\left(\frac{d_\theta}{N}+d_x\right).\]<p>The parameter contribution is divided by particle count, while one latent-dimension contribution remains. This is scalar normalization of the same-reference entropy, not a change of measure. Assumption \(\beta_N=N\alpha\) must hold for the interacting lifted target.</p>''',[
ref('IPLA/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean','particle_kl_bound_normalized_of_finite_kl'),
ref('IPLA/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean','particle_kl_bound_linear_lsi_of_finite_kl')],
'The complete joint estimate is divided by particle count before the linearly scaled lifted constant is substituted.'),
part('parameter','Parameter-law relative entropy by data processing',r'''<p>Let \(P_\theta\) be parameter projection as in <cite data-report-ref="IPLA/Geometry.lean" data-report-declaration="parameterCLM"></cite> and define \(\mu_k^\theta=P_{\theta\#}\mu_k\) and \(\Pi_N^\theta=P_{\theta\#}\Pi_N\). Measurable data processing gives</p>\[H_{\Pi_N^\theta}(\mu_k^\theta)\le H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_k).\]<p>For \(\beta_N=N\alpha\), substitution of the full entropy estimate yields</p>\[H_{\Pi_N^\theta}(\mu_k^\theta)
\le e^{-\alpha\gamma k}H_{\Pi_N}(\mu_0)
+\frac{8\gamma NL^2}{\alpha}\left(d_x+\frac{d_\theta}N\right).\]<p>The initial quantity remains full joint entropy. The projected initial entropy may be smaller, which does not permit its replacement in this upper bound. A per-particle estimate must first be multiplied by \(N\). Joint finiteness gives projected finiteness before the real-valued comparison.</p><p>For density \(R=d\mu_k/d\Pi_N\), its marginal density is \(\mathbb E_{\Pi_N}[R\mid\theta]\). Conditional Jensen for convex \(u\log u\) gives</p>\[\int\mathbb E[R\mid\theta]\log\mathbb E[R\mid\theta]d\Pi_N
\le\int R\log R\,d\Pi_N.\]<p>There is no factor \(N^{-1}\) in data processing. Hence normalization of a joint entropy bound must be undone by multiplication by \(N\) before it bounds the ordinary parameter divergence.</p>''',[
ref('IPLA/LSI/UnconditionalTheorem.lean','parameter_kl_bound_of_finite_kl')],
'Data processing bounds the probability marginal divergence. It does not establish concentration about a likelihood maximizer.'),
]


